---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import Header from "../components/Header.astro";
import Footer from "../components/Footer.astro";

const title = "Why Our Concrete Lasts | 5-Inch Slabs and 21AA | Faber Concrete";
const description =
  "Why Faber Concrete pours five-inch slabs on compacted 21AA instead of four inches over sand: Michigan cold, heavy trucks, and a finish from crews with 25-plus years.";
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={description}>
  <Header />
  <main class="section">
    <div class="wrap prose">
      <p class="eyebrow">The spec</p>
      <h1>Why our concrete lasts</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Two decisions do most of the work: an extra inch of concrete, and a real base under it. We do it this way every time. We do not cut the corner.
      </p>

      <h2>Five inches, not four</h2>
      <p>
        We never pour a four-inch slab. Five inches, every time, no matter the job. Six inches on the approach. That is an inch more than what most contractors pour.
      </p>
      <p>
        They do it because it is cheaper. Add it up and the money they save by leaving out that inch is probably $600 to $700 on average. Faber Concrete charges the customer for the inch. The bid will not be the cheapest one. It is the highest-quality bid, priced down the middle, with a better finish.
      </p>
      <p>
        If you are looking for a cheap deal, this is not it. That is what Matt tells people when he gives the estimate.
      </p>

      <h2>Cold climate, heavy trucks</h2>
      <p>
        Michigan winters are why the extra inch is on the whole driveway, not just a patch. Freeze and thaw work on a thin slab. So does traffic that the old four-inch rule never really planned for.
      </p>
      <p>
        Picture a 20-ton delivery truck — the kind that brings a package to the house — rolling in and out of the driveway. The approach takes that load. Six inches there, five everywhere else, is the thickness we pour so the slab is still a slab after those seasons, not a cracked surface you resent but have not replaced yet.
      </p>
      <div class="callout">
        The crack you live with is the expensive one. It is not always bad enough to tear out this year. It is bad enough that the driveway stops feeling like it was done right.
      </div>

      <h2>Compacted 21AA, before the pour</h2>
      <p>
        Thickness does not save a slab that is sitting on the wrong ground. We never pour over dirt. We always pour over 21AA.
      </p>
      <p>
        The base is compacted every time, before forming and before the five-inch pour. You are not only getting the extra inch. You are getting a compacted 21AA base that makes the slab more solid, with less chance of the dirt or sand underneath eroding out.
      </p>
      <p>
        The shortcut on the other bid is familiar: deliver sand, compact the sand, pour over the sand, and save a few hundred dollars. We take 21AA, grade the whole area, compact the whole area, and then pour five inches over it. That is the way it is supposed to be done.
      </p>

      <h2>The finish</h2>
      <p>
        Our finishers have been finishing for over 25 years. The finish process is part of the premium product — the surface you actually live with — on top of a body of concrete that was not thinned out to win the price.
      </p>

      <div class="actions">
        <a class="btn btn--tide" href="/estimate">Request an estimate</a>
        <a class="btn btn--ghost" href="/services/driveways">Driveways</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </main>
  <Footer />
</BaseLayout>
